  "The Office: Dunder Mifflin": {
    ACCOUNTING: "The accounting department — Angela, Oscar and Kevin — sits together in a corner of the office, where Angela runs a tight ship.",
    ANNEX: "The small back room of the office, home to Kelly and Toby, and later to others.",
    BALER: "The warehouse machine that crushes waste cardboard into tight bundles — one of the heavy machines the warehouse crew handles downstairs.",
    BOSS: "Michael sees himself as a great boss — just look at his proudly displayed “World’s Best Boss” mug.",
    BRANCH: "Dunder Mifflin is split into regional branches. The show follows the Scranton branch, which is often under threat of being closed.",
    BREAKROOM: "Where the office gathers to eat lunch, gossip and avoid work — and where the vending machine lives. Many of the show’s quiet side conversations happen here.",
    BUFFALO: "Another Dunder Mifflin branch, in upstate New York. Like Scranton, it’s one of the regional offices that has to fight to survive as the company struggles.",
    CALL: "Sales calls are the salespeople’s daily grind. Jim, Dwight, Stanley, Phyllis and the others spend their days on the phone with clients.",
    CHAIR: "In “The Chair Model,” Michael falls for a model from an office-furniture catalogue.",
    CLIENT: "Winning and keeping clients is everything for Dunder Mifflin’s salespeople, especially as big chains try to undercut them.",
    COPIER: "As a paper company, Dunder Mifflin’s office is full of copiers and paper. The copy room is a frequent spot for gossip and pranks.",
    CORPORATE: "Dunder Mifflin’s head office in New York City, home of Jan, David Wallace and, for a while, Ryan.",
    DEAL: "Closing a deal is the goal of every sales call. Dwight often holds the office record for sales.",
    DESK: "Jim and Dwight’s facing desks are the battleground for many pranks — Jim once wraps Dwight’s entire desk in gift paper.",
    DUNDER: "Robert Dunder, one of the two founders of Dunder Mifflin. The company’s name joins its founders’ surnames: Dunder and Mifflin.",
    EMAIL: "In “Email Surveillance,” Michael decides to monitor everyone’s emails and discovers he wasn’t invited to Jim’s party.",
    FAX: "One of Jim’s most famous pranks: he sends Dwight faxes from “Future Dwight,” warning him about things that are about to happen.",
    FILE: "Filing and paperwork are the dull backbone of office life — and the perfect thing for Michael to procrastinate on.",
    INK: "Ink cartridges, toner and supplies are part of what Dunder Mifflin sells alongside its paper.",
    LOBBY: "The building’s front lobby and elevators, where Hank sits at the security desk.",
    MANAGER: "Michael is regional manager — and Dwight is assistant regional manager, or as Michael insists, “Assistant to the Regional Manager.”",
    MEETING: "Michael’s meetings in the conference room are infamous for being long, pointless and wildly inappropriate.",
    MEMO: "Office memos are a constant, especially from corporate — and Michael loves to make his own important announcements.",
    MIFFLIN: "Robert Mifflin, the other founder of Dunder Mifflin, alongside Robert Dunder.",
    NASHUA: "Dunder Mifflin’s branch in Nashua, New Hampshire, where Holly is transferred, which breaks Michael’s heart.",
    PAPER: "Dunder Mifflin is a paper company fighting to survive in an increasingly paperless world.",
    PEN: "Pens, pencils and other basic supplies are part of the office-supply range Dunder Mifflin sells alongside its paper.",
    PHONE: "Pam answers the phones at reception: “Dunder Mifflin, this is Pam.”",
    PITCH: "A sales pitch to a client. Michael is surprisingly great at selling, even when he’s terrible at everything else about his job.",
    PRINTER: "After Sabre buys Dunder Mifflin, it pushes its printers — some of which turn out to catch fire.",
    PROFIT: "The Scranton branch often turns out to be one of the company’s most profitable, despite Michael’s management.",
    QUOTA: "Salespeople have monthly sales targets to hit. Dwight usually beats his and makes sure everyone knows it.",
    RECEPTION: "The front desk, staffed first by Pam and then by Erin.",
    SABRE: "The printer company led by Jo Bennett that buys Dunder Mifflin in season 6.",
    SALES: "The sales team is the heart of the office. Jim, Dwight, Andy, Stanley and Phyllis all work in sales.",
    SCRANTON: "Scranton, Pennsylvania, where the show is set and where the Dunder Mifflin branch is located.",
    STAMFORD: "The Connecticut branch where Jim transfers in season 3, before it merges with Scranton.",
    STAPLER: "In the very first episode, Jim puts Dwight’s stapler in a bowl of Jell-O.",
    TAPE: "Office supplies like tape and binders are part of the endless stock that keeps the office running.",
    UTICA: "The New York branch managed by Karen, often a rival to Scranton.",
    VENDING: "The breakroom vending machine, a fixture of office snack breaks.",
    WAREHOUSE: "Downstairs, where Darryl, Roy and the warehouse crew load trucks and keep the paper moving.",
  },

