  "Artificial Intelligence": {
    AGENT: "An AI that can take actions toward a goal, such as searching the web or running code, rather than only answering.",
    AI: "Artificial intelligence: computer systems that do tasks we associate with human thinking. The term was coined for a 1956 workshop at Dartmouth College.",
    ALGORITHM: "A step-by-step set of instructions for solving a problem. The word comes from the name of the 9th-century mathematician al-Khwarizmi.",
    ALIGNMENT: "The work of making AI systems behave in line with human values and intentions.",
    ALPHAGO: "DeepMind’s program that beat Go champion Lee Sedol in 2016 — a game long thought too intuitive for computers.",
    ATTENTION: "A technique that lets a model focus on the most relevant parts of its input. It powers the transformer, introduced in 2017.",
    BENCHMARK: "A standard test used to measure and compare how well AI systems perform.",
    BIAS: "When an AI’s outputs are unfairly skewed, often because of imbalances in its training data.",
    CAPTCHA: "Those “prove you’re human” puzzles on websites. The name stands for a public Turing test to tell computers and humans apart.",
    CHATBOT: "A program you can talk to in everyday language. Today’s chatbots are powered by large language models.",
    CHIP: "Specialised AI chips are designed to do the huge numbers of calculations neural networks need.",
    CODE: "The instructions that make up a program. AI tools can now help write and review code.",
    DATA: "The examples an AI learns from — text, images, sound. Its quality shapes what the model learns.",
    DATASET: "A collection of examples gathered to train or test an AI system.",
    DEEPBLUE: "IBM’s chess computer that beat world champion Garry Kasparov in a match in 1997.",
    ELIZA: "One of the first chatbots, built in the 1960s by Joseph Weizenbaum. It imitated a therapist by rephrasing what you said.",
    EMBEDDING: "A list of numbers representing the meaning of a word or image, so similar things end up close together.",
    EPOCH: "One complete pass through the whole training dataset. Models are often trained for several epochs.",
    FINETUNE: "Further training a ready-made model on a smaller, specialised dataset to adapt it to a particular task.",
    GPU: "Graphics processing units were built for video games, but their ability to do many calculations at once made them the engines of modern AI.",
    INFERENCE: "Using a trained model to make a prediction or produce an answer, as opposed to training it.",
    LABEL: "The correct answer attached to a training example, like “cat” on a photo of a cat.",
    LAYER: "Neural networks are built in layers, each transforming the information a bit more. “Deep” learning means many layers.",
    LLM: "A large language model: a neural network trained on vast amounts of text to understand and generate language.",
    LOGIC: "Early AI tried to capture intelligence in rules of logic. Modern AI mostly learns from examples instead.",
    LOSS: "A score of how wrong a model’s predictions are. Training works by nudging the weights to make the loss smaller.",
    MODEL: "The trained system at the heart of an AI: a huge set of learned numbers that turns inputs into outputs.",
    NETWORK: "In AI, a network of simple connected units that together can learn very complex patterns.",
    NEURAL: "Neural networks are loosely inspired by the brain’s neurons, passing signals through layers of connected units.",
    OVERFIT: "When a model memorises its training examples instead of learning general patterns, so it does badly on new data.",
    PARAMETER: "Another name for the adjustable numbers inside a model. Bigger models usually have more of them.",
    PATTERN: "Machine learning is about finding patterns in data that are too subtle or complex to spell out by hand.",
    PERCEPTRON: "An early, very simple artificial neuron, invented by Frank Rosenblatt in 1958 — an ancestor of today’s networks.",
    PREDICT: "At heart, many AI models predict: the next word, the next frame, the most likely label.",
    PROMPT: "The instructions or question you give an AI. How you phrase it can change the answer a lot.",
    PYTHON: "The most popular programming language for AI. It’s named after Monty Python, not the snake.",
    ROBOT: "A machine that can sense, decide and act. The word comes from robota, “forced labour”, in Karel Čapek’s 1920 play R.U.R.",
    SENSOR: "Cameras, microphones and lidar give robots and self-driving cars their view of the world.",
    SPEECH: "Speech recognition turns spoken words into text; speech synthesis turns text into a natural-sounding voice.",
    TOKEN: "A chunk of text, often part of a word, that a language model reads and writes one at a time.",
    TRAINING: "The process of adjusting a model by showing it many examples, so its predictions slowly get better.",
    TURING: "Alan Turing, the British mathematician who in 1950 proposed the Turing test: can a machine’s conversation pass for a human’s?",
    VECTOR: "A list of numbers. AI represents words, images and ideas as vectors so it can compare them mathematically.",
    VISION: "Computer vision lets machines recognise faces, read signs and find objects in images.",
    WEIGHTS: "The numbers inside a neural network that are tuned during training. Large models have billions of them.",
  },
